docs/guide/mcp.md:425:| `make_vertical_version` | A 9:16, 4:5 or 1:1 version of the top-level timeline (**File ▸ Make Vertical Version…**): the canvas keeps the short side, each full-frame movie clip follows its subject (`follow`, `zoom`, `where_empty`, as `reframe_clip`) — a screen recording with a cursor file follows its cursor instead, its own zooms and pans replaced, or is shown whole when the file cannot be read; with `follow: speaker` and `mics` (two to four clips, each standing for its track as one person's microphone) a wide shot of two to four people playing beside two or more of them cuts to whoever is talking, as `reframe_clip`'s `speaker` frames it, its faces matched by Frame Job or named with `mic_faces`, and every other movie clip follows its faces; a camera and its screen in camera layouts are re-laid for the new canvas (Side by Side as Stacked on a tall one; treatment `relaid`, or `not_followed` with the hand-edit reason when a layout was moved by hand) — stills and nests are centred, a graphic exactly the old canvas's size is re-sized to the new one (treatment `resized`; its position by the two sides' ratios, its scale kept), titles, pictures-in-picture, shapes and other graphics are kept as placed, full-frame plates re-filled and old blurred copies removed or re-filled. By default it opens a new project holding the change as one undo step "Make Vertical" (saved first at `path` when given; the project it comes from does not change); `copy: false` applies it to the project you name instead. It analyses the movie clips on this Mac first and answers `analyzing` past 60 s, making nothing. Word caption tracks are re-paged for the new canvas as `relay_captions` does, and the answer's `captions` lists those re-laid and those kept with their reasons; `captions: false` leaves caption tracks as placed and drops the key. In a copy, the project's own older copies of the bundled lower-third, title-card, callout, bar-chart, counter and kinetic-type templates are rewritten with the current text (the old text kept as a revision); in place they are only listed; the answer's `graphics` is `{resized, updated, listed [{name, reason}]}`, a module sizing itself by the canvas's height listed too. Answers each clip's treatment and reason, the counts, `cursor` (the clips followed by their cursor), `relaid` (each re-laid switch's `camera`, `at`, `from`, `to` and a corner's `shape`), `speaker` (each clip framed on whoever is talking, with its `mics` and `mic_faces`) and a summary. The assistant asks first when it names a `path`. |
docs/guide/mcp.md:535:| `reframe_clip` | *op* — frames one clip on the current canvas: `follow` `subject`, `face`, `person` or `salient` follows what is found in the clip on this Mac (the tool analyses its window and waits up to 60 s; past that it answers `analyzing` and changes nothing — call it again), `center` holds the window on the picture's centre at `zoom` (1–4 × fill), `fit` shows the whole picture, `keep` rescales from `from_width × from_height`; `cursor` follows a screen recording's recorded pointer from its media's cursor file (read at once, no analysis, no wait), arriving on each click and holding still while typing, and a clip whose media has a cursor file is refused by the face-following kinds; `layout` re-lays a camera clip's camera layouts and its screen's on the current canvas from `from_width × from_height`, refused naming a switch edited by hand; `speaker` frames a wide shot on whoever is talking, from the microphone clips in `mics` (read as Switch by Speaker reads them; each matched to the face whose mouth moves with it, or named with `mic_faces`, faces numbered left to right), a cut at each change held at least 2 s, two at once framed together when they fit, refused naming each face's place when the match is weak; written as the clip's position and scale and one action per move, with a blurred copy on `backdrop` behind any span that shows the whole picture. A clip with actions is refused, unless `keep` and none moves or scales it ([Vertical and framing](vertical-and-framing.md)). |
